<h2>How the course runs</h2>
<p>Each session covers one module. Read the module's notes, and ideally the matching chapter, before class: the session
  opens with a short quiz on it, then works through the key derivations and code together. The exercises at the end of
  each module are the best preparation for the quizzes: try each derivation yourself before checking it against the
  notes, and change the code to see what breaks.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>In-class quizzes (50%)</strong> — one short quiz at the start of each of the ten module sessions.</li>
  <li><strong>Participation (15%)</strong> — working through derivations and code in class, and questions and
    discussion.</li>
  <li><strong>Final project (35%)</strong> — described below; the last two sessions are the project talks.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Final project</h2>
<p>
  One project for everyone: <strong>take a real classification problem, build several classifiers for it from
  scratch, and answer a sharp question about why they perform as they do</strong>, using the tools of the whole course.
  Implement at least three classifiers from different modules in NumPy — for example a Gaussian Bayes classifier, a
  nearest-neighbor rule, and a linear machine, network, or tree — estimate each one's error with cross-validation and
  a confidence interval, and compare them with a paired test. Then pick one question and investigate it carefully:
  does performance peak as features are added, does editing the training set help nearest neighbors, when does
  boosting beat bagging, how far is each classifier from an estimate of the Bayes error? The project is written up as
  a short research paper with reproducible code, and presented in the final two sessions.
</p>

<h2>Getting set up</h2>
<p>The code in the notes needs Python 3 with NumPy and SciPy; nothing else. Any recent Python installation, or a free
  <a href="https://colab.research.google.com/" rel="noopener">Google Colab</a> notebook in the browser, runs every
  example. Paste the cells of a module into a notebook in order and run them: every random number comes from a seeded
  generator, so your output should match the notes.</p>
